ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [Framing-corruption -> silent truncate is the *documented* design choice of any length-prefixed/terminator-framed log: the parser cannot distinguish "torn tail from an in-flight batch" from "torn middle from a lost page," so it conservatively truncates at last_valid_pos. The location-asymmetry is therefore not an emergent property but a direct consequence of (a) per-batch checksum scope = data only, and (b) framing-loss being indistinguishable from a normal incomplete trailing write. This is folklore-adjacent (RocksDB kPointInTimeRecovery, LevelDB log_reader, etcd/raft WAL all have the same shape).]
CONFOUNDS: [Default PersistMode::Buffer is a no-op fsync, so "SyncAll returned Ok" is the ONLY path that even claims durability; the dm-flakey result drops un-fsync'd Buffer data which is contractually allowed loss, not a bug. The faithful reorder-SIM is a SIM on real bytes, not a real reordered device write — it cannot distinguish "fjall violates its durability contract" from "we zeroed a page the device would never have lost post-fsync." Selection of 50 keys + single-page zero is a chosen failure mode, not a sampled one.]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [(1) That SyncAll's fsync barrier is actually crossable by a real reordered writeback on a real block device (needs dm-log-writes barrier replay — explicitly not done, this is the L1 gap the orchestrator itself flags). (2) That xxh3 over batch-data-only is a design defect rather than a deliberate scope choice (no comparison to RocksDB/LevelDB framing-checksum scope). (3) That "clean open, no Err" is unexpected for a length-framed log after a hole (most WAL recoverers do exactly this by design). (4) No test of whether enabling PersistMode::SyncData/SyncAll + a real post-fsync reorder still silently truncates.]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [n=1 trial per corruption mode (single 50-key run, single page zeroed, single offset for each of mid_truncate/mid_zero/mid_flip/early_trunc); no variance, no repetition, no seed sweep over WHERE the hole lands or HOW MANY post-hole batches exist; "49/50, 25/50, 16/50, 2/50" are point estimates with no CI; no comparison run on a peer LSM (RocksDB/sled/redb) with the SAME injection harness to establish whether the asymmetry is fjall-specific or universal — without that, the "fjall-specific" novelty claim is unsupported by the evidence in the packet.]
RATIONALE: The mechanism is real and source-verified (writer.rs:232 no-op + per-batch xxh3 scope + truncate_to(last_valid_pos) on framing desync), and the in-data LOUD control vs framing SILENT discriminator is a clean falsifier that fired correctly. That earns it above RED. But the headline framing — "location decides loud-vs-silent, clean open while discarding committed data" — is the *expected* behavior of any length-framed log with data-scoped checksums, and the packet itself concedes the durability-contract boundary (B) is not crossed: the only demonstrated silent losses are (i) un-fsync'd Buffer data (documented loss, not a bug) and (ii) a SIMULATED dropped page on real bytes (not a real post-fsync reorder). Until a dm-log-writes barrier-replay shows fjall silently discarding data whose SyncAll fsync ALREADY returned Ok on a real device, the severity is "fjall's framing recovery is silent where some peers are loud" — interesting and worth a fjall issue, but not a publishable silent-acked-loss finding. Also missing: peer-LSM comparison under identical injection (to substantiate "fjall-specific"), repetition/variance on every cell of the asymmetry table, and a search for the exact location-asymmetry framing in LevelDB/RocksDB/etcd recovery docs (cite: RocksDB WAL recovery modes, LevelDB log_reader::ReadPhysicalRecord truncation behavior) before claiming un-owned. YELLOW: advance to L1 with the dm-log-writes barrier-reorder replay AND a peer-LSM differential; if post-fsync silent loss reproduces on real hardware, this flips GREEN; if only the Buffer-mode + sim results stand, it stays YELLOW (real but bounded by documented contract).
